CAPTIONS={
1:dict(title='MHW-intensity forecast errors in time and space',role='Complementary intensity metric and regional error structure',
caption='a, Event-mean root-mean-square error (RMSE) of MHW intensity against peak Niño3.4 anomaly. The line fits nine earlier El Niño events; grey shading is the 95% prediction interval for an additional event. The red star denotes 2023–2024, excluded from fitting. b, The 404-month error series; shaded periods mark 1997–1998, 2015–2016 and 2023–2024. c, Increase in intensity RMSE relative to the equal-event mean squared error of the two earlier strong events. Red denotes larger errors; blue denotes smaller errors; grey ocean cells lack a valid comparison. The inset summarizes the four basins selected in main Fig. 1c, with all nine lead values and their range. RMSE is calculated only where an MHW is observed; false alarms elsewhere are excluded. Smaller RMSE denotes better intensity prediction, not necessarily better occurrence prediction. The map retains the 1° grid, ±2 °C scale and 20° basin divisions of main Fig. 1c. Aggregation, coverage and source-selection limits are specified in Supplementary Methods and Fig. 16.',
statistics='The global series combines monthly spatial mean squared errors before taking the root. Regional values pool gridpoint mean squared errors on a fixed common-cell mask; they are not spatial averages of the RMSE-difference map. Lead ranges are not confidence intervals.'),
2:dict(title='Associations between ENSO phase and MHW forecast skill',role='ENSO-phase association and dependence-aware uncertainty',
caption='a,b, Global MHW area under the ROC curve (AUC) against absolute Niño3.4 anomaly during El Niño and La Niña months, respectively. The thresholds are +0.5 °C and −0.5 °C; neutral months are outside this comparison. Lines are ordinary-least-squares fits and r is the monthly Pearson correlation. Both panels use the same AUC axis. The displayed 95% correlation intervals are percentile intervals from 2,000 whole-calendar-year resamples; the same sampled years are used for both phases when estimating the slope difference. Months within years and successive ENSO months are dependent; they are not independent events. Year-block resampling addresses within-year dependence but may not capture the full duration of multiyear ENSO episodes.',statistics='All phase-eligible months are retained; no independent-month P values or multiple-comparison significance symbols are used.'),
3:dict(title='Sensitivity of the amplitude–skill relationship to year and event selection',role='Small-event-sample robustness',
caption='a, Mean AUC versus midpoint year for the nine earlier El Niño events. b, Niño3.4 amplitude and AUC after removing their respective linear relationships with event year. c, The Niño3.4 coefficient in AUC = intercept + amplitude coefficient × Niño3.4 + year coefficient × event year, refitted after omitting each named event. Each point uses eight events; the red dashed line is the coefficient from all nine events. The 2023–2024 event is excluded throughout. These nine omission estimates describe sensitivity to event selection; their spread is not a confidence interval and they are not nine independent experiments. Adjustment for event year assesses linear temporal confounding only.',statistics='Nine historical events; deterministic ordinary-least-squares fits. Event amplitudes and AUCs are identical to main Fig. 1a.'),
4:dict(title='MHW skill shortfalls across lead months and within events',role='Lead and target-month robustness of main Fig1',
caption='a, AUC expected from earlier events (dark line) and realized in 2023–2024 (red line), evaluated separately at each lead from 1 to 9 months. Connecting vertical segments show the expected-minus-realized difference. At each lead, AUC is averaged over July–June and regressed on El Niño amplitude using the nine earlier events; 2023–2024 is excluded. b, AUC averaged over leads 1–9 for each verifying month in the three strong events. The light-grey, dark-grey and red lines denote 1997–1998, 2015–2016 and 2023–2024. All 1,080 event–month–lead records are used: ten events × twelve months × nine leads. Leads and months are repeated evaluations, not independent events.',statistics='No lead-specific significance test is claimed. These panels retain the forecast extraction and monthly spatial-AUC definition of main Fig. 1a,b.'),
5:dict(title='Tropical forecast correlations and sensitivity to the earlier event',role='Display all tropical diagnostic values and comparison dependence',
caption='a,c,e, Forecast–observation spatial correlations for SST, zonal wind stress and precipitation, respectively. Precipitation is the measured proxy for convection. Darker blue denotes stronger spatial agreement. b,d,f, Percentage correlation decrease in 2023–2024 relative to 1997–1998, 2015–2016 or their equal-event mean: 100 × (reference correlation − 2023–2024 correlation) / reference correlation, calculated at each displayed lead label. Positive values indicate lower correlation in 2023–2024. Grey and dark-grey curves use one earlier event each; red uses their mean. All 81 coefficients underlying main Fig. 2 are retained. The underlying extraction uses variable-specific windows, model/member availability and nearest-lead selection; the nine labels do not certify nine exact common future leads. Supplementary Methods and Table 1 distinguish this diagnostic from the exact-coordinate DJF analysis in Figs. 6–9.',statistics='There are three events, with nine repeated diagnostic labels per variable. The curves compare two references; they are not confidence bounds. Percentages have separate denominators and cannot be added as contributions to MHW skill loss.'),
6:dict(title='Observed and forecast 200-hPa circulation during three strong El Niño events',role='Observed/forecast/error field transparency',
caption='Rows show December–February (DJF) of 1997–1998, 2015–2016 and 2023–2024; columns show ERA5 200-hPa geopotential-height anomalies, multimodel forecast anomalies and forecast minus observed anomalies. Forecasts first average members within each model, then equally weight the three available models and the eight common future leads. Observed and forecast fields share the ±160 m colour scale; differences share ±100 m, with extensions marking values beyond the limits. Red denotes positive height anomalies or positive forecast error, blue negative values. Anomalies use calendar-month means from 1992–2020; February 2018 is omitted consistently from all model and observation climatologies because the corresponding GEOS-S2S field is missing. No event DJF month is excluded. Lead 9 is absent from the fixed three-model cohort. Maps retain their native 1° resolution without added smoothing.',statistics='Models are COLA–RSMAS–CCSM4, NASA–GEOS-S2S and GFDL–SPEAR. These are seasonal composites, not isolated causal ENSO responses. Signed averaging can cancel opposing errors; Fig. 8 also evaluates RMSE.'),
7:dict(title='Regional circulation forecast skill across lead months',role='Regional and lead heterogeneity with explicit missingness',
caption='a–c, Forecast–observation spatial correlations of DJF 200-hPa height anomalies in 1997–1998, 2015–2016 and 2023–2024. d, Correlation in 2023–2024 minus the equal-event mean of the two earlier events. Panels a–c share the −1 to 1 scale; in d, red denotes lower and blue higher correlation. Grey cells labelled NA denote unavailable lead 9, not zero skill; 48 region–lead combinations are valid per event. NP, North Pacific; SP, South Pacific; TA, tropical Atlantic; NA, North Atlantic; SA, South Atlantic; IO, Indian Ocean. Here NA on a row label is North Atlantic, whereas NA inside a cell means not available. Pacific and Atlantic masks use ±23.5° and ±60° boundaries as in main Figs. 3–4; tropical Pacific is the source and is omitted from this six-recipient comparison. The full boundaries are in the coverage table.',statistics='These six ocean-mask diagnostics are not four idealized PNA/PSA/Indian/Atlantic waveguide boxes. No significance is inferred from the number of leads or grid cells.'),
8:dict(title='Model dependence of circulation forecast agreement and RMSE',role='Model robustness and non-cancelling error check',
caption='a, Global spatial correlation of forecast and observed DJF 200-hPa height anomalies. b, Corresponding area-weighted RMSE. Each row is one model or the equal-weight multimodel mean. Colours denote 1997–1998 (light grey), 2015–2016 (dark grey) and 2023–2024 (red). Large points show the mean over eight leads; small points retain all lead values; whiskers span their minimum and maximum. The multimodel mean is formed from fields before verification. The domain is all longitudes, 60°S–60°N, including land, weighted by cosine latitude. NCEP–CFSv2 is absent because 1997–1998 200-hPa data were unavailable in the common source set used for main Fig. 3; it is assessed separately in Fig. 14.',statistics='The plot includes 72 single-model and 24 multimodel event–lead evaluations from three events. Lead ranges are not confidence intervals. RMSE squares spatial errors before averaging and is therefore not reduced by cancellation of positive and negative errors.'),
9:dict(title='Event dependence of the tropical–remote forecast relationship',role='Separate between-event and within-event associations',
caption='a–c, Tropical Pacific precipitation forecast–observation correlation versus remote 200-hPa height forecast–observation correlation, shown separately for the three events. Symbols identify the six recipient regions defined in Fig. 7. Each panel contains all 48 region–lead pairs; r summarizes their within-event association. d, The pooled association for all events and after omitting each event. The pooled relationship compares both differences between events and differences within events, whereas a–c assess the latter alone. Each tropical lead value is repeated for six recipients. Thus 144 plotted pairs represent three event clusters, not 144 independent events; the weak within-event associations cannot establish a stable predictive relationship or one-way transmission of forecast error.',statistics='All values, including negative circulation correlations, are retained. Event omission is a sensitivity analysis, not out-of-event prediction validation. No independent-sample P value is reported.'),
10:dict(title='Reference-event dependence, anomaly amplitude and skill against climatology',role='Distinguish pattern similarity from amplitude and climatology performance',
caption='a, Global anomaly correlations of the 2023–2024 forecast (red) and the forecast composite of the two earlier strong events (dark grey) against each of six other El Niño winters, identified by start year. These are events in the main Fig. 1 list within the historical-reference window 1992–1993 to 2021–2022, excluding 1997–1998 and 2015–2016; 1991–1992 is outside that window. b, Forecast-to-observed spatial standard-deviation ratio of height anomalies. c, Skill against a zero-anomaly forecast, defined as 1 − MSE_forecast/MSE_zero-anomaly. Values above zero indicate lower error than climatology. Panels b,c use 30°–60°N and 40°–60°S along the sector from 120°E eastward to 20°E. The dashed line in b marks equal forecast and observed amplitude.',statistics='Individual-event correlations are not equivalent to correlation with the averaged observed field. A smaller forecast anomaly amplitude does not by itself establish better or worse forecast skill. No confidence intervals are estimated for these deterministic comparisons.'),
11:dict(title='Longitudinal structure of midlatitude height anomalies and forecast errors',role='Locate longitudinal displacement and signed error beyond global metrics',
caption='a,c, Observed (solid) and forecast (dashed) DJF 200-hPa height anomalies along 45°N and 50°S. Dark grey denotes the equal-event composite of 1997–1998 and 2015–2016; red denotes 2023–2024. b,d, Forecast minus observed height along the same sections. All 360 longitude nodes per section are retained, starting at 120°E and continuing eastward around the globe. The two latitudes sample the northern and southern midlatitude circulation to illustrate differences in the longitude and strength of anomaly centres; they do not identify fixed Rossby-wave trajectories or the seasonally most active waveguide. Positive errors indicate heights predicted too high and negative errors heights predicted too low, relative to the observed field, irrespective of the sign of the observed anomaly.',statistics='Forecasts average the eight common leads. Anomalies and source fields are identical to Fig. 6. The focus is longitudinal error structure, not a new estimate of global MHW skill.'),
12:dict(title='Historical SST associations and their local-process components',role='Make statistical weights and denominators transparent',
caption='a, Allocation of historical regional SST variance among ENSO, other climate modes, prior ocean thermal state, surface heating, horizontal advection and an unresolved fraction. The denominator is total SST variance. b, The three local components divided by their combined share, so the denominator is the local-group allocation, not total variance. Prior thermal state comprises SST and mixed-layer temperature one month earlier. Other modes comprise IOD, PDO, NAO and SAM indices. Both panels use 432 monthly records per region during 1985–2020, with the ±23.5° masks of main Fig. 4. Seasonal means and a linear trend are removed before group-respecting variance allocation. Shared explanatory information is averaged over predictor-group orders, with nested ordering within the local group.',statistics='These are statistical SST associations, not heat-budget fractions or measured contributions of process-specific forecast errors in 2023–2024. Their connection to main Fig. 4b is the explicitly defined weighting rule in Supplementary Methods.'),
13:dict(title='Out-of-period diagnostic SST reconstruction',role='Temporal transfer and predictor-removal sensitivity',
caption='This analysis uses contemporaneous observed climate indices, surface heating and horizontal advection, together with prior SST and mixed-layer temperature; it is diagnostic reconstruction, not an operational forecast. a, Later-period regional SST reconstruction using climate indices alone or all measured predictors. b, Skill decrease after removing each predictor group from the full model; negative values indicate improved reconstruction after removal. Three forward test blocks are 2003–2008, 2009–2014 and 2015–2020. Each model is trained only on earlier months beginning in 1985; seasonal terms, linear trend, scaling and coefficients are fitted within training data. Reconstruction skill is 1 − (model SSE / baseline SSE), where SSE is the sum of squared errors and the baseline is the training-fitted seasonal cycle plus linear trend. Squared errors are summed across test blocks before taking the ratio.',statistics='All seven regions and seven predictor configurations are retained. The three successive test blocks are not independent ENSO events. This score is not MHW AUC and does not validate the causal interpretation of the Sankey diagram.'),
14:dict(title='CFSv2-only consistency check of circulation and surface heat-flux errors',role='Separately processed single-model process consistency',
caption='a, CFSv2 circulation RMSE versus net surface heat-flux RMSE, each divided by its own 1997–1998/2015–2016 mean at the same region and lead. Colours and symbols distinguish the three events. The dashed line is the pooled fit. b, Percentage RMSE increases in 2023–2024 relative to those earlier-event means, averaged over leads 1, 3 and 6 months. Red denotes greater error and blue smaller error. c, Correlations between the two normalized errors after omitting each event; the red dashed line denotes the pooled estimate. All masks now use the ±23.5° divisions of main Figs. 3–4. The 54 remote pairs comprise three events × six recipient regions × three leads; nine additional tropical-Pacific pairs enter b.',statistics='This is a separately downloaded and processed CFSv2-only check, not an independent model: CFSv2 also contributes to the main SST ensemble. Circulation is departure from contemporaneous zonal mean, verified against ERA5; net heat flux is verified against ORAS5 and is positive into the ocean. Four members and three DJF months are equally weighted. The circulation metric differs from the anomaly correlation in main Fig. 3; no causal or independent-event significance is inferred.'),
15:dict(title='Signed regional accounting underlying the main AUC-gap diagram',role='Exact mathematical and numerical correspondence to main Fig4',
caption='a, Regional allocations of the historical-expected minus observed global AUC difference, using the same seven regions, ±23.5° boundaries and ranking-pair definition as main Fig. 4b. Other seas contains evaluated cells outside those seven masks. Positive values add to lower-than-expected global skill; negative values reduce the net difference. b, Positive and negative amounts assigned to each component using the same historical SST association weights as main Fig. 4b. Hatched bars show the negative amounts. Prior thermal state, surface heating and horizontal advection subdivide the local group, which is not added again. All amounts are in AUC units; dividing their net sum by 0.1090497372 gives the percentages in the main diagram.',statistics='The expected global AUC is 0.7050185255 and observed AUC 0.5959687883. The baseline is the nine-event amplitude regression, not the mean of the two strong events. Regional AUCs are not summed: individual positive–negative ranking comparisons are allocated to regional endpoints. Closure is mathematical, not predictive or causal validation; the unresolved category is the unallocated SST association weight, not a fitted process-error residual.'),
16:dict(title='Regional skill changes and the ocean area supporting their comparison',role='Show missing spatial support and all seven regional results',
caption='a,c, Regional AUC decreases and MHW-intensity RMSE increases in 2023–2024 relative to 1997–1998 and 2015–2016. Bars show the pooled regional difference; points retain all nine leads and whiskers span their range. b,d, Fraction of each analysis region meeting the common-cell validity requirements for the respective metric in all three events and all nine leads. Regional results describe only those valid cells, not the entire basin. Temporal AUC is undefined where the twelve verifying months contain only one observed class; conditional intensity RMSE requires observed-MHW months. Missing values are never replaced by zero or smoothed into neighbouring cells.',statistics='These panels preserve the 20° regional divisions and post-hoc selection framework of main Fig. 1c and Supplementary Fig. 1c; the process diagnostics in Figs. 7 and 12–15 instead follow the updated ±23.5° divisions of main Figs. 3–4. The two masks are not interchangeable. The map diagnostic is temporal AUC across July–June, whereas main Fig. 1a,b use monthly spatial AUC. The respective regional differences cannot be added to recover the global AUC gap.')
}

METHODS=[
('Scope of the supplementary analyses','These analyses address three questions: whether MHW forecast performance differs from historical expectations; how tropical and remote atmospheric forecasts differ; and how regional SST associations enter the diagnostic allocation of the global AUC difference. Figures 1–4 and 16 assess MHW intensity, occurrence and sampling coverage; Figs. 5–11 examine tropical and remote atmospheric forecasts; Figs. 12–15 evaluate regional SST associations, their temporal transfer, a single-model process comparison and the global AUC accounting. Supplementary Table 1 identifies the data and sampling support of each analysis.'),
('Events, repeated samples and uncertainty','The earlier strong events are 1997–1998 and 2015–2016; the focal event is 2023–2024. The nine-event amplitude regression additionally uses 1991–1992, 1994–1995, 2002–2003, 2004–2005, 2006–2007, 2009–2010 and 2018–2019. Month, lead, model and spatial-grid replication do not create additional independent ENSO events. In Fig. 2, the same 2,000 resampled calendar-year blocks are used jointly for both phases, yielding percentile intervals for correlations and their slope contrast. Prediction intervals in Fig. 1 describe a new event under the linear regression assumptions. Lead and historical-reference ranges elsewhere are descriptive ranges, not confidence intervals. No new multiple-comparison significance claims are made.'),
('AUC and conditional intensity errors','Monthly global AUC ranks forecast threshold exceedance against observed MHW/non-MHW status across ocean cells, with cosine-latitude weights. Main Fig. 1c instead computes temporal discrimination at each cell across July–June, separately by lead; cells without both classes are undefined. Forecast and observed MHW intensity are max(SST − monthly threshold, 0). Conditional intensity MSE is evaluated only at observed-MHW cells, excluding false alarms elsewhere. Global intensity summaries first average monthly spatial MSEs across the retained leads and months, then take a square root. Spatial and regional intensity comparisons first form cellwise MSEs on a fixed valid-cell support; the canonical reference is the square root of the equal-event mean MSE. Neither regional RMSE differences nor regional temporal AUC differences are additive global-AUC contributions. Figure 16 reports the resulting support limitation without filling missing cells.'),
('Forecast extraction shared with the main SST figures','Figures 1–4, 15 and 16 retain the archived SST extraction used in main Figs. 1 and 4. That extraction averages stored initialization coordinates in some selected forecast files. A file-level audit has identified cases where filename-based and exact initialization-coordinate selections differ, including GFDL files for September and December 2023 with conflicting source-coordinate attribution. Consequently these supplementary SST panels describe the retained analysis, not an independently initialization-controlled validation. Exact-coordinate extraction changes the regression-based global deficit in the separately available sensitivity analysis; it is not silently substituted here. This source limitation must be resolved together with the main-text SST analysis before submission claims of robust magnitude are made.'),
('Tropical correlation comparisons','Figure 5 reproduces the 81 coefficients underlying main Fig. 2. They describe the 20°S–20°N, 140°E–100°W tropical Pacific. SST uses DJF anomaly patterns; atmospheric diagnostics retain a two-calendar-year extraction with a final-December timestamp mismatch (23 observed months) and legacy model/member averaging. Wind stress at nominal label 9 uses a nearest available lead where an exact lead is absent. The source coefficient table does not document a complete valid-model count for every atmospheric coefficient. Thus nine labels do not certify nine exact common leads, and the three variables do not form a matched-window, matched-model coupling experiment. Relative decreases are 100 × (r_reference − r_target)/r_reference; nonpositive reference correlations would be undefined, not replaced by an arbitrary denominator. Figure 5 tests reference dependence of this retained diagnostic; it does not cure its input-selection limitations.'),
('Exact-coordinate DJF circulation comparisons','Figures 6–11 use DJF targets and exact available future leads 1–8 for COLA–RSMAS–CCSM4 (members 1–10), NASA–GEOS-S2S (1–4) and GFDL–SPEAR (1–15). Equal weights are applied to members within a model, then to model fields. All required fields must be finite. NCEP–CFSv2 is not in this fixed source set because its 1997–1998 Z200 product is unavailable there; NASA limits the common exact lead range to 1–8. Each calendar-month climatology spans 1992–2020, excluding February 2018 uniformly after the corresponding NASA field failed the availability check, leaving 29 December, 29 January and 28 February samples. No target-event DJF month is removed. The spatial correlation centers each field before cosine-latitude-weighted covariance; RMSE retains amplitude and sign-independent error magnitude. Pooled tropical–remote associations include between-event differences; their within-event counterparts cannot be substituted for them.'),
('Region definitions','For main Figs. 3–4 and Supplementary Figs. 7 and 12–15, NOAA WOA major-basin identity is intersected with latitude bands: TP/TA, 23.5°S–23.5°N; NP/NA, 23.5°N–60°N; SP/SA, 60°S–23.5°S. IO spans the Indian Ocean from 60°S to its northern boundary. Grid-centre membership assigns −23.5° to the tropical band and +23.5° to the northern band, with no overlap. NP, SP, TA, NA, SA and IO are the six atmospheric recipient ocean masks. Global circulation comparisons additionally include land. The earlier main Fig. 1c spatial skill analysis and its Supplementary Figs. 1c and 16 use the retained ±20° split; they are shown explicitly as a different support, not silently relabelled as the process regions.'),
('Historical SST association and reconstruction','Figures 12–13 use regional ERSST SST and ORAS5 mixed-layer temperature, surface heating and horizontal advection. The fitting record is January 1985–December 2020 (432 months). ENSO is a separate group; IOD, PDO, NAO and SAM form the other-mode group. Local variables are previous-month SST and mixed-layer temperature, contemporaneous net surface heating divided by mixed-layer heat capacity, and horizontal advection. Full-sample month effects and linear trend are removed for the in-sample variance allocation. Group Shapley values average marginal explained variance over group orders; nested Owen values split the local parent while preserving group membership. The unresolved fraction is 1 − full-model R². All shares sum to one. For forward reconstruction, the seasonal cycle, trend, predictor scaling and regression are trained only before each test block. Test-time contemporaneous observed predictors make this diagnostic reconstruction rather than an actionable forecast.'),
('CFSv2-only process check','Figure 14 uses a separately retrieved CFSv2 process archive, four members (00, 06, 12 and 18), DJF and leads 1, 3 and 6 months. Verification uses ERA5 upper-level circulation and ORAS5 net ocean surface heat flux. The circulation quantity is the departure from contemporaneous zonal mean, not the calendar-month anomaly used in main Fig. 3. Net surface flux is positive downward into the ocean. Squared errors are averaged across the three months and cosine-latitude-weighted common ocean cells before taking the root. Each RMSE is divided by the mean of the two earlier-event RMSEs at the same region and lead. The regional masks have been recomputed at ±23.5°. CFSv2 is also one component of the main SST ensemble; this is therefore a separately processed single-model consistency check, not a model-independent validation of the mechanism.'),
('Exact relationship between Supplementary Fig. 15 and main Fig. 4','For each global positive–negative ranking comparison, its weighted AUC contribution is divided equally between the two regional endpoints, including Other seas. These endpoint amounts sum exactly to the global AUC; separately calculated basin AUCs generally do not. The same nine-event regression weights are applied to each regional endpoint amount, and the target amount is subtracted, yielding D_b. If w_b,k is a historical SST association weight, the displayed component allocation is C_b,k = D_b × w_b,k. Summing over k recovers D_b and summing over b recovers the retained global deficit, 0.1090497371807756. Negative allocations reduce the positive amounts; absolute-value normalization is not used. Dividing each net amount by this global deficit and multiplying by 100 gives the main Sankey percentages. The seven-region and component crosswalks supplied in the source tables match main Fig. 4 v23 exactly. Closure follows from the definitions and is not evidence of causal process attribution.'),
]

